\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Label transport and topology}

\p{A sequence of puzzle moves may return pieces to their positions while
changing their orientations. The first question is what local return map
records that change; the \vocabk{cycle criterion}{frap-000U} computes it.
A related question asks how labels move between neighboring simplices. This
chapter uses the \newvocab{projectivity} construction of Izmestiev and
Joswig to make that question precise, then records the topology of one
complete unfolding \citet{Sections 2.1--2.3.3}{izmestiev2002branched}.}

\subtree{
  \title{Perspectivities along facet paths}

  \p{Let #{K} be a pure #{d}-dimensional simplicial complex. If neighboring
  facets #{\sigma} and #{\tau} share a ridge, #{\sigma} has one vertex
  #{v(\sigma,\tau)} not in #{\tau}. The \newvocab{perspectivity}}
  ##{h_{\sigma,\tau}:V(\sigma)\longrightarrow V(\tau)}
  \p{swaps that exceptional vertex with #{v(\tau,\sigma)} and fixes the
  common ridge vertices. For a facet path
  #{\gamma=(\sigma_0,\ldots,\sigma_n)}, its \newvocab{projectivity} is the
  composite}
  ##{\langle\gamma\rangle=h_{\sigma_0,\sigma_1}\cdots
      h_{\sigma_{n-1},\sigma_n}.}
  \p{A closed facet path based at #{\sigma_0} gives a permutation of
  #{V(\sigma_0)}. The set of these permutations is the
  \newvocab{projectivity group} #{\Pi(K,\sigma_0)\leq\operatorname{Sym}(V(\sigma_0))};
  changing the base facet conjugates the group when the dual graph is
  connected. The inverse path supplies the inverse permutation, so this is
  transport with a genuine composition law.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Coloring as a trivial-transport test}

  \p{A proper coloring of the vertices of #{K} with #{d+1} colors gives
  every facet a consistently labeled vertex set. Each perspectivity preserves
  those labels, so every projectivity is the identity. Thus proper coloring
  is a sufficient condition for #{\Pi(K)=1}; a nontrivial projectivity group
  is an obstruction to such a global labeling
  \citet{Section 2.1}{izmestiev2002branched}.}

  \p{This is a useful comparison with a framed puzzle. A local frame assigns
  labels in one fiber, and a move transports them to another fiber. The
  \vocabk{framed transport}{frap-000Q} and its cycle criterion
  \vocabk{abelianized character}{frap-000S} measure the return action in the
  puzzle model. Projectivities do the analogous bookkeeping for simplex
  vertices, but the two constructions have different state spaces and move
  laws. No identification between their groups is being assumed.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Complete unfolding as a transport cover}

  \p{Fix a base facet and a labeling of its vertices by #{0,\ldots,d}. A
  facet reached by a path inherits a labeling by transporting along that
  path. Different paths can give different admissible labelings, precisely
  when their projectivity differs. The \newvocab{complete unfolding} keeps
  these admissibly labeled facets as separate sheets and glues neighboring
  sheets only when their labels agree on the common ridge. It carries a
  canonical map back to #{K}; its projectivity group acts by permuting the
  sheets \citet{Section 2.2}{izmestiev2002branched}.}

  \p{The construction is therefore a topological record of transport
  failure: a loop in the base may close geometrically while changing the
  sheet label. The complete unfolding removes that ambiguity by remembering
  the transported labeling. This is analogous to a covering-space monodromy
  calculation; the branch locus is additional local-combinatorial data of
  the unfolding and is not inferred here from the puzzle model.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{The tetrahedral boundary example}

  \p{For the boundary complex #{\partial\Delta^3}, the projectivity group is
  #{S_3}. Its complete unfolding is glued from 24 triangles. Identifying
  opposite sides of the triangulated hexagon gives a torus #{T^2}; the
  canonical map to the tetrahedral boundary, topologically #{S^2}, is a
  six-fold branched covering. There are four branch points on #{S^2}; each
  has three preimages, each of branching index two
  \citet{Section 2.3.3, Figure 3}{izmestiev2002branched}.}

  \p{The example separates three pieces of data that are easy to conflate:
  the base triangulation, the permutation group generated by facet-path
  transport, and the topology of the unfolded surface. The torus is not a
  claim about a cube solver. It is a concrete model showing how local label
  transport can assemble a global branched cover.}
}

\p{For FRAP, the practical lesson is to name the fiber, the local transport
map, and the allowed paths before comparing topology with puzzle orientation.
The projectivity example supplies a precise transport vocabulary; the
framed cube account supplies a different finite-state calculation. A future
formal bridge would need an explicit map between those data and a proof that
it preserves composition, return maps, and the claimed topology.}
